From: buntOS contributors
Subject: [PATCH] usb: typec: tcpci_mt6375: get through a hard reset from the source

A hard reset from the source left this port dead until the plug or the
driver was pulled, with the input of the charger at the USB default. A
PPS source sends one when requests stop coming, and any source can.

On receiving a hard reset the chip puts ALERT_MASK and POWER_STATUS_MASK
back to their defaults and clears RECEIVE_DETECT. The rest of its
registers stay as they were. That goes wrong in three steps:

 - FAULT_STATUS says ALL_REG_RST_TO_DEFAULT from the reset in
   mt6375_tcpc_init(), as tcpci_init() clears it just before calling
   that. With the FAULT alert now unmasked the interrupt line stays up,
   and the PD event of the chip comes on an edge, so no alert gets here
   any more. TCPM runs out of tries and waits in SNK_UNATTACHED for
   good.

 - With the fault cleared by hand, the next alert finds
   POWER_STATUS_MASK at 0xff and tcpci_irq() has TCPM start over, in the
   middle of its recovery from the hard reset.

 - Starting over runs mt6375_tcpc_init() again, and its software reset
   also masks the PD event on the PMU side of the chip: 0x1ff goes from
   0xfe back to 0xff. regmap-irq does not look again, so alerts then
   only arrive when another interrupt of the chip happens to read the
   event. TCPM ends up with the wrong polarity, no messages and a limit
   of 0 mA.

So clear the fault after the reset, put the masks back at the start of
the interrupt handler unless the fault status says that the chip was
reset, and reset the chip only while the interrupt is not in use yet.

On the device with a 68 W charger in PPS and no more requests: hard
reset 14.1 s after the last one, VBUS gone 30 ms later and back after
0.84 s, Source_Capabilities after another 170 ms, and the contract back
1.14 s after the hard reset, through SNK_HARD_RESET_SINK_OFF, _WAIT_VBUS
and _SINK_ON as TCPM means it. Seven times out of seven. Before this it
took a new plug.

---
 drivers/usb/typec/tcpm/tcpci_mt6375.c | 50 +++++++++++++++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 47 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/usb/typec/tcpm/tcpci_mt6375.c b/drivers/usb/typec/tcpm/tcpci_mt6375.c
index 09cf181..87ca3a5 100644
--- a/drivers/usb/typec/tcpm/tcpci_mt6375.c
+++ b/drivers/usb/typec/tcpm/tcpci_mt6375.c
@@ -163,9 +163,26 @@ static int mt6375_tcpc_init(struct tcpci *tcpci, struct tcpci_data *data)
 	u8 mask[MT6375_NUM_VEND_INT] = { };
 	int ret;
 
-	ret = mt6375_tcpc_reset(regmap);
-	if (ret)
-		return ret;
+	/*
+	 * Not once the interrupt is in use. The reset also masks the PD event
+	 * on the PMU side of the chip, where regmap-irq does not look again,
+	 * and alerts then stop for good.
+	 */
+	if (!priv->irq_ready) {
+		ret = mt6375_tcpc_reset(regmap);
+		if (ret)
+			return ret;
+
+		/*
+		 * tcpci_init() cleared this before the reset set it. Left set
+		 * it keeps the FAULT alert up, which holds the interrupt line
+		 * high once a hard reset has unmasked that alert.
+		 */
+		ret = regmap_write(regmap, TCPC_FAULT_STATUS,
+				   TCPC_FAULT_STATUS_ALL_REG_RST_TO_DEFAULT);
+		if (ret)
+			return ret;
+	}
 
 	/* Rd on both, at once: a USB host must not see the pins open long */
 	ret = mt6375_tcpc_set_rd(regmap);
@@ -312,6 +329,31 @@ static int mt6375_tcpc_set_vbus(struct tcpci *tcpci, struct tcpci_data *data,
 	return 1;
 }
 
+/*
+ * A hard reset from the source puts the alert masks back to their defaults,
+ * with the rest of the chip as it was. tcpci_irq() takes that for a chip that
+ * lost its state and has TCPM start over, in the middle of the recovery from
+ * the hard reset. So put them back first. A reset of the chip says so in the
+ * fault status, and stays for tcpci_irq() to find.
+ */
+static void mt6375_tcpc_keep_masks(struct regmap *regmap)
+{
+	unsigned int fault;
+	u16 mask;
+
+	if (regmap_raw_read(regmap, TCPC_ALERT_MASK, &mask, sizeof(mask)) ||
+	    mask == MT6375_ALERT_MASK)
+		return;
+
+	if (regmap_read(regmap, TCPC_FAULT_STATUS, &fault) ||
+	    (fault & TCPC_FAULT_STATUS_ALL_REG_RST_TO_DEFAULT))
+		return;
+
+	mt6375_tcpc_write16(regmap, TCPC_ALERT_MASK, MT6375_ALERT_MASK);
+	regmap_write(regmap, TCPC_POWER_STATUS_MASK,
+		     TCPC_POWER_STATUS_VBUS_PRES);
+}
+
 static irqreturn_t mt6375_tcpc_irq(int irq, void *dev_id)
 {
 	struct mt6375_tcpc *priv = dev_id;
@@ -319,6 +361,8 @@ static irqreturn_t mt6375_tcpc_irq(int irq, void *dev_id)
 	irqreturn_t ret;
 	bool present;
 
+	mt6375_tcpc_keep_masks(priv->tcpci_data.regmap);
+
 	ret = tcpci_irq(priv->tcpci);
 
 	/*
